@*
    The shell (spec §3.7): one app bar, one nav drawer, the pending bar under the app bar, and the bottom bar under
    720 px.

    The two layouts are one markup tree, not two components. A second component would be a second navigation — and
    §4.3 argues at length that a second navigation is how a navigation starts lying about what works. That is also
    why the drawer's entries are SectionLink rather than the library's nav link (V8): a section here owns routes
    beyond its own prefix (Schema owns /changes, Data the record routes), and the library's link cannot say so.
*@
@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject AssistantGateway Assistant
@inject ILogger<AdminLayout> Logger
@inject AdminInterop Interop
@inject IBrowserViewportService Viewport

@*
    The library's four providers, in the interactive layout because the library does not render them statically
    (study §1.3). Exactly one popover provider: a second throws. Dialog defaults are the pattern language's:
    focus lands on the first field, and a click on the scrim never dismisses (spec §3.1-3.2). Each wrapper still
    states its own options.
*@
<AlvoTheme />
<MudPopoverProvider />
<MudDialogProvider DefaultFocus="DefaultFocus.FirstChild" BackdropClick="false" CloseButton="false" />
<MudSnackbarProvider />

<MudLayout Class="a-shell">
    <MudAppBar Dense="true" Elevation="0" Class="a-appbar" data-testid="appbar">
        <AlvoButton @ref="_sectionsButton" Tone="AlvoButton.ButtonTone.Ghost" Small="true" class="a-only-mobile" aria-label="Sections"
                    aria-expanded="@(_navOpen ? "true" : "false")" data-testid="more-sections" OnClick="_ => ToggleNav()">
            <Icon Path="@Icons.More" />
        </AlvoButton>
        <a class="a-brand" href="@AdminPaths.Overview" aria-label="Alvo">
            <img src="@AlvoAdminAssets.Mark" width="26" height="26" alt="" />
            <span>Alvo</span>
        </a>
        <AlvoButton Tone="AlvoButton.ButtonTone.Ghost" Small="true" aria-label="Search" OnClick="_ => OpenPalette()">
            <Icon Path="@Icons.Search" />
            <span>Search</span>
            <kbd class="a-kbd">&#8984;K</kbd>
        </AlvoButton>
        <span class="a-spacer"></span>
        @*
            Mounted only when a connection resolves. A deployment with no AI gets no launcher, no badge and no
            explanation to read: spec §3.3, a control whose only possible outcome is "not configured" is worse than
            its absence, which is the argument Settings already makes about issuing an API key. An icon alone on a
            phone, named by its label.
        *@
        @if (_assistant)
        {
            <AlvoButton @ref="_assistantLauncher" Tone="AlvoButton.ButtonTone.Ghost" Small="true" data-testid="assistant-launch"
                        aria-label="Ask Alvo" aria-expanded="@(_assistantOpen ? "true" : "false")" OnClick="_ => ToggleAssistant()">
                <Icon Path="@Icons.Sparkle" />
                <span class="a-only-desktop-block">Ask Alvo</span>
            </AlvoButton>
        }
        <ThemeToggle />
        <SignedInAs />
    </MudAppBar>

    @*
        The persistent drawer on a desktop, a temporary one over the page on a phone, where the bottom bar carries
        the first five sections and the app bar's Sections button opens the rest. The variant follows Alvo's own
        720 px phone width, not the library's responsive breakpoints (600 and 960 px): those would draw a third
        layout between them and 720, which the stylesheet does not have.

        No MudNavMenu around the list. Its entries are SectionLink (V8), so it added only a second, unlabelled
        <nav> inside this one: a navigation landmark with no name, and nothing else.
    *@
    <MudDrawer Open="_navOpen" OpenChanged="OnDrawerOpenChanged"
               Variant="@(_phone ? DrawerVariant.Temporary : DrawerVariant.Persistent)"
               ClipMode="DrawerClipMode.Always" Elevation="0" Class="a-navdrawer">
        <nav aria-label="Sections" data-testid="sidebar" class="a-navdrawer__nav" tabindex="-1" @ref="_drawerNav">
            <ProjectSwitcher />
            <NavList OnNavigate="CloseNavOnPhone" />
        </nav>
    </MudDrawer>

    <MudMainContent Class="a-main">
        <PendingBar />

        <main class="a-content" id="a-content">
            @*
                A screen that throws while rendering takes this panel instead of the circuit. Without the
                boundary a render bug ends the session: the shell, the working copy's pending count and every
                other screen go with it, and the operator reads the framework's reconnect banner. The
                boundary's own logger writes the exception to the server log, so the panel can say "in the
                server log" and mean it.
            *@
            <ErrorBoundary @ref="_boundary">
                <ChildContent>@Body</ChildContent>
                <ErrorContent Context="fault">
                    <ErrorPanel Fault="@fault">
                        <Actions>
                            <AlvoButton Small="true" data-testid="error-reload" OnClick="_ => Reload()">Reload</AlvoButton>
                        </Actions>
                    </ErrorPanel>
                </ErrorContent>
            </ErrorBoundary>
        </main>

        @* The pane's last row rather than the layout's, so it sits under the content on a phone and needs no
           height of its own subtracted anywhere. *@
        <nav class="a-bottomnav" aria-label="Sections" data-testid="bottom-nav">
            @foreach (var entry in AdminNavigation.Bar)
            {
                @SectionLink.For(entry, "a-bottomnav__item", "a-bottomnav__item--active", BarContent(entry))
            }
        </nav>
    </MudMainContent>

    @if (_assistant)
    {
        @*
            Beside the page, not over it (spec §3.1): a persistent end drawer at every width, with no scrim, no scroll
            lock and no focus trap, and labelled so a screen reader reaches it as a region. Clipped under the app bar,
            and the page column narrows beside it, so neither the header nor the pending bar is covered. On a phone
            it is the whole width below the app bar. Kept mounted while closed, because the thread lives in the
            component and closing the pane must not end the conversation.
        *@
        <MudDrawer Open="_assistantOpen" Anchor="Anchor.End" Variant="DrawerVariant.Persistent"
                   ClipMode="DrawerClipMode.Always" Elevation="0" Width="min(440px, 100vw)"
                   Class="a-assistant-drawer" role="complementary" aria-label="Ask Alvo">
            <CascadingValue Value="AssistantPane">
                <AssistantDrawer />
            </CascadingValue>
        </MudDrawer>
    }
</MudLayout>

<CommandPalette @ref="_palette" />

@code {
    private CommandPalette? _palette;
    private ErrorBoundary? _boundary;
    /// <summary>The widest viewport that is a phone: alvo.css's one phone breakpoint, and the drawer's.</summary>
    private const int PhoneWidth = 720;

    private static readonly string _phoneQuery = $"(max-width: {PhoneWidth}px)";

    /// <summary>Two breakpoints, either side of the phone width, so the viewport reports crossing it and no other.</summary>
    private static readonly MudBlazor.Services.ResizeOptions _phoneCrossing = new()
    {
        NotifyOnBreakpointOnly = true,
        BreakpointDefinitions = new() { [Breakpoint.Xs] = 0, [Breakpoint.Sm] = PhoneWidth + 1 },
    };

    private readonly Guid _viewportWatch = Guid.NewGuid();
    private AlvoButton? _sectionsButton;
    private AlvoButton? _assistantLauncher;
    private EventCallback _closeAssistant;
    private bool _assistantOpen;
    private ElementReference _drawerNav;
    private IAsyncDisposable? _dismiss;
    private FocusNext _focusNext;
    private bool _assistant;
    private bool _navOpen = true;
    private bool _phone;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether to mount the assistant, asked at boot and again whenever the connection is saved.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>Every refusal is "no launcher", never a failed page.</b> The shell renders for a caller the
    /// project names nowhere and for one whose account has been deleted since they signed in — and both
    /// reach this call. A layout that let either throw would replace the screen that says what happened
    /// with a 500, which is measured rather than imagined: it took out the revoked-session scenario.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>Asked again, because once per circuit was a bug rather than a simplification.</b> An operator who
    /// configured the connection on Settings got a panel reporting <c>configured</c> and a shell with no
    /// launcher in it, for the rest of the session and with nothing on screen suggesting a reload. The
    /// subscription is to <see cref="AssistantGateway.ConnectionChanged"/>, which the save raises — see the
    /// argument for the event there.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _closeAssistant = EventCallback.Factory.Create(this, CloseAssistant);
        Assistant.ConnectionChanged += OnConnectionChanged;
        await RefreshAssistantAsync();
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        Assistant.ConnectionChanged -= OnConnectionChanged;

        if (_dismiss is not null)
        {
            await _dismiss.DisposeAsync();
        }

        await StopWatchingViewportAsync();
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            await StartAsync();
        }

        await MoveFocusAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Learns the width, follows it and Escape, then says the shell has rendered interactively, providers and all.
    /// It is the readiness signal a scenario waits on before it opens a popover or a dialog (study §5.1 gotcha 2):
    /// a click before the circuit is live is lost, and so is a phone's first choice in the drawer made before the
    /// shell knows it is on a phone.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task StartAsync()
    {
        await Viewport.SubscribeAsync(_viewportWatch, OnViewportChanged, _phoneCrossing, fireImmediately: false);
        ApplyWidth(await Viewport.IsMediaQueryMatchAsync(_phoneQuery));
        _dismiss = await Interop.SubscribeAsync("dismiss", _ => InvokeAsync(DismissDrawer));
        StateHasChanged();
        await Interop.MarkShellReadyAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>The viewport crossed the phone width, one way or the other.</summary>
    private Task OnViewportChanged(BrowserViewportEventArgs change) => InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        ApplyWidth(change.BrowserWindowSize.Width <= PhoneWidth);
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>
    /// A phone's drawer is the temporary one and starts closed; a desktop's is the persistent sidebar and starts
    /// open. Only a change of side resets it, so a resize within one side keeps what the operator chose.
    /// </summary>
    private void ApplyWidth(bool phone)
    {
        if (phone == _phone)
        {
            return;
        }

        _phone = phone;
        _navOpen = !phone;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Lets the viewport go, unless the circuit already has: a layout disposed with its circuit has no browser left
    /// to stop listening in.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task StopWatchingViewportAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await Viewport.UnsubscribeAsync(_viewportWatch);
        }
        catch (Exception exception) when (exception is JSDisconnectedException or ObjectDisposedException
                                              or OperationCanceledException)
        {
            /* Nothing is left to unsubscribe from. */
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Clears a caught fault on every navigation — the layout's parameters are set again with each new page —
    /// so the panel belongs to the screen that threw rather than to the session.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnParametersSet() => _boundary?.Recover();

    /// <summary>Draws the screen again from scratch, which re-reads everything it shows.</summary>
    private void Reload() => _boundary?.Recover();

    /// <summary>
    /// Re-asks, then redraws — on the renderer's thread, because the save that raised this ran on the
    /// settings page's own.
    /// </summary>
    private void OnConnectionChanged() =>
        _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
        {
            await RefreshAssistantAsync();
            StateHasChanged();
        });

    /// <summary>The one place the question is asked, so the boot and the re-ask cannot answer it differently.</summary>
    private async Task RefreshAssistantAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            _assistant = await Assistant.IsConfiguredAsync(CancellationToken.None);
        }
        catch (Exception exception)
        {
            AdminProblem.Absorb(exception, Logger);
            _assistant = false;
        }

        _assistantOpen &= _assistant;
    }

    /// <summary>What the pane reads: whether it is open, and the close it asks for.</summary>
    private AssistantPane AssistantPane => new(_assistantOpen, _closeAssistant);

    /// <summary>The launcher opens the pane or closes it; the pane moves focus into itself as it opens.</summary>
    private void ToggleAssistant()
    {
        if (_assistantOpen)
        {
            CloseAssistant();
            return;
        }

        _assistantOpen = true;
    }

    /// <summary>Closes the pane, from its close button, Escape inside it, or the launcher, and focus goes back to the launcher.</summary>
    private void CloseAssistant()
    {
        _assistantOpen = false;
        _focusNext = FocusNext.Launcher;
    }

    private Task OpenPalette() => _palette?.OpenAsync() ?? Task.CompletedTask;

    /// <summary>Opens or closes the phone's drawer; opening it takes focus into it (spec §3.4).</summary>
    private void ToggleNav()
    {
        _navOpen = !_navOpen;
        _focusNext = _phone && _navOpen ? FocusNext.Drawer : FocusNext.None;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A choice in the drawer closes it on a phone, where it covers the page; on a desktop it is the persistent
    /// sidebar and stays. Focus is the new screen's, which the router moves to its heading.
    /// </summary>
    private void CloseNavOnPhone()
    {
        if (_phone)
        {
            _navOpen = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The library closed the phone's drawer, on a click on its scrim: focus goes back to Sections.</summary>
    private void OnDrawerOpenChanged(bool open)
    {
        _navOpen = open;
        _focusNext = !open && _phone ? FocusNext.Sections : FocusNext.None;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Escape, forwarded from alvo.js: it closes the phone's drawer and gives focus back to Sections. The library's
    /// temporary drawer does neither, and a drawer over the page is the topmost thing Escape closes (spec §3.4).
    /// </summary>
    private void DismissDrawer()
    {
        if (!_phone || !_navOpen)
        {
            return;
        }

        _navOpen = false;
        _focusNext = FocusNext.Sections;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>Moves focus where the last open or close of the drawer said it goes, once the render has drawn it.</summary>
    private async Task MoveFocusAsync()
    {
        var next = _focusNext;
        _focusNext = FocusNext.None;

        if (next == FocusNext.Drawer)
        {
            await _drawerNav.FocusAsync();
        }
        else if (next == FocusNext.Sections && _sectionsButton is not null)
        {
            await _sectionsButton.FocusAsync();
        }
        else if (next == FocusNext.Launcher && _assistantLauncher is not null)
        {
            await _assistantLauncher.FocusAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Where focus goes after the next render.</summary>
    private enum FocusNext
    {
        /// <summary>It stays where it is.</summary>
        None,

        /// <summary>Into the drawer that just opened.</summary>
        Drawer,

        /// <summary>Back to the Sections button that opened the drawer that just closed.</summary>
        Sections,

        /// <summary>Back to the launcher of the assistant's pane that just closed.</summary>
        Launcher,
    }

    /// <summary>A bottom-bar entry: a smaller icon over the section's short title.</summary>
    private static RenderFragment BarContent(AdminSection entry) => @<text>
        <Icon Path="@entry.Icon" Size="18" />
        <span>@entry.ShortTitle</span>
    </text>;
}
